<Lecture
  chapterSlug="08-cli"
  lessonSlug="04-wc-clone"
  hook="A clone of Unix wc. Five functions: three counters, one orchestrator, one parser."
>
  <h2>The five functions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>count_lines(path)</code> — int</li>
    <li><code>count_words(path)</code> — int</li>
    <li><code>count_chars(path)</code> — int (includes newlines)</li>
    <li><code>count_all(path)</code> — tuple, calls the three above</li>
    <li><code>parse_args(argv)</code> — argparse with path + 3 flags</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout tone="warning" title="🔒 no_paste mode is ON">
    <p>Graded homework. Tutor maxes at L2.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The shape</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`import argparse
import sys

def count_lines(path):
    with open(path) as f:
        return sum(1 for _ in f)

def count_words(path):
    with open(path) as f:
        return sum(len(line.split()) for line in f)

def count_chars(path):
    with open(path) as f:
        return sum(len(line) for line in f)

def count_all(path):
    return (count_lines(path), count_words(path), count_chars(path))    # composition

def parse_args(argv):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Count lines, words, characters.")
    parser.add_argument("path", help="Path to the file to count")
    parser.add_argument("--lines", action="store_true")
    parser.add_argument("--words", action="store_true")
    parser.add_argument("--chars", action="store_true")
    return parser.parse_args(argv)

# In Pyodide, demonstrate with an in-memory file:
with open("demo.txt", "w") as f:
    f.write("the quick brown fox\\njumps over the lazy dog\\n")

print(count_all("demo.txt"))     # (2, 9, 45)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Decomposition is graded">
    <p>
      The autograder verifies that <code>count_all</code> CALLS at least 2
      of the 3 single-counters. Inlining is rejected — you have to compose.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Acceptance criteria</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All 8 tests pass</li>
    <li>Each counter uses <code>with open(...)</code></li>
    <li><code>count_all</code> calls at least 2 of the 3 counters</li>
    <li><code>parse_args</code> accepts <code>path</code> + <code>--lines</code> / <code>--words</code> / <code>--chars</code></li>
  </ul>
</Lecture>
